  "schema": 1,
  "scope": "The whole original server (every Kotlin and Java file under Server/src/main, compiled unchanged with Kotlin 1.8.20 and javac on JDK 11 against the dependencies its POM names) booted in one JVM by the original ServerConfigParser.parse and GameWorld.prompt(false), then driven by a script: bytes handed to the session's own event producers, MajorUpdateWorker.handleTickActions(false) called once per tick, IoSession.disconnect for a closed connection. Recorded per step: bytes each session was sent, repository lists, tick counter, generator positions, files written. Not original: the socket (three IoSession members), the 600 ms worker loop and its ping-timeout, inactive-player and midnight-restart checks, Server.main's networking, console reader and watchdog, and the SystemManager.flag call that starts the worker. Pinned inputs: wall clock and unseeded java.util.Random constructions (call sites redirected after compilation; census in this file), RandomFunction.RANDOM, Math.random, Collections.shuffle and the world thread's ThreadLocalRandom (replaced or seeded), Dispatchers.Default and the management-event queue (run on the world thread when the harness pumps), identity hash codes (one constant for every object, -XX:hashCode=2, so hash collections keyed by identity iterate in an order fixed by their insertion history), JVM flags, locale and time zone. The configuration is worldprops/default.conf with enable_bots=false. Class-scan order, directory listing order and anything else that depends on the JDK build or operating system are not controlled individually: they are observed to repeat (two fresh JVM runs must agree byte for byte before a fixture is written or accepted) on this JDK build and operating system only. No native world replays this trace yet; it establishes nothing about the Rust server.",
